Z||000000||BBBAD GT||03-09-2000||10:21 AM||||Seems like we are beating up this topic to death, but I've assembled a price list below detailing what an 88 GA brake swap would possibily cost.  I'd like opinions on the brands and if anybody knows where they might be significantly cheaper.  I like the deal Carparts.com is doing, get $30 off on your first order of $100.  I can order some with my CC and work address, and my wife can order some with our home address and her CC.  Opinions welcome please.  Shipping wasn't factored in below.  I hope the margins come out on this, if not, I think you can figure it out tho.<P>Proposed 1988 Grand Am Brake Swap<P><BR>			Price		Core		Brand<BR>Front (Carparts.com):<BR>Rotors (2)		23		0		Raybestos<BR>L Loaded Caliper		38-40		10-20		Cardone/AC Delco Durastop/Wagner<BR>R Loaded Caliper		38-40		10-20		Cardone/AC Delco Durastop/Wagner			<BR>$30 off when order $100	(30)				My first order at Carparts.com<P>Hubs/bearings/seals	110		90	 <A HREF="http://members.xoom.com/_XMCM/jrisagod/brakekit1.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.xoom.com/_XMCM/jrisagod/brakekit1.htm</A> <BR>			<BR>	<BR>Rear (Carparts.com):<BR>Rotors (2)		23		0		Raybestos<BR>L Loaded Caliper		38-40		10-20		Cardone/AC Delco Durastop/Wagner<BR>R Loaded Caliper		38-40		10-20		Cardone/AC Delco Durastop/Wagner			<BR>$30 off when order $100	(30)				Wife's first order at Carparts.com <P>Total			256		170<P>Options:<P>SS lines bk & ft		90				Fiero Store<BR>S10/Balzer MC		50				Anywhere<BR>Hurst Line Lock		100				Summit<BR>Fluid			10				Anywhere<P>Total			506		170<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||03-09-2000||12:17 PM||||Actually, those prices look pretty good for the most part. I was thinking I paid less than that for my calipers though. I called my "buddies" down at Advance Auto, they had Bendix OEM rebuilt calipers for about $27 plus core charge (which I think is what I used for calipers and I think I used Raybestos for the rotors).<P>They also had the cheapy Cardone calipers for almost half as much, but I've heard mixed results with that brand. You'd be better off with Raybestos or Bendix, in my batty opinion. The advantage with a local store is that you can bring in about any junk caliper for the core. I keep my old Fiero calipers for future use on one of my other Fieros.<P>I believe there are a couple Forum members that might be willing to turn your rotors down into hubs cheaper then what they're charging on that website you posted. I paid $50 plus shipping for my hubs.<P>Also, I noticed you forgot to calculate in the brake pads (you sort of need those too because the old Fiero pads won't work).<P>One last thing, I know nothing about line locks except that they're used in drag racing for burnouts, BUT in an old topic here on the Forum, that was discussed as an alternative for the e-brake. Somebody cited reasons why this was not recommended, but I've forgotten why. Maybe do a search or reask the line lock question again.<P>Sounds like you're on the right path to doing a major brake upgrade. You may also consider replacing the wheel bearings and seals while everything is apart, cheap insurance. Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||BBBAD GT||03-09-2000||12:56 PM||||I always thought 'loaded' calipers came with pads?  At least in the description, it says what kind of pads are on it.  Thanks for the Carbone tip, I see a couple alternatives on loaded calipers: one being an AC Delco/Durastop with semi-metallic pads for $40 and $20 charge, and the other a Raybestos loaded one also with semi metallic pads (its says 'original assy') for $38 and $20 core.  It might just be easier to go with Raybestos for the rotors/calipers/pads combo.  Was going to replace all the bearings and seals up front, not too sure if there are bearing in the back to replace (permanent bearings?).<P>I got a line loc on my Mustang, and yes, they are used for Drag racing (make perfect burnouts).  They also work great if you are stopped on a hill with a clutch and don't want to mess with the ebrake.  It isn't permanent like an ebrake is after you stop the car from running. All I need is to find the line that goes to the front brakes, and put the loc inbetween.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||03-09-2000||01:06 PM||||Ok, if the "loaded" caliper means with pads, then that accounts for the higher price and why you didn't list brake pads. I like and use the semi-metallic pads. Sounds like you're definitely getting your guano together. It's good to do your homework before spending the big bucks. Before I did my swap, I was burning up the Forum with my brake swap questions too.<P>The rear wheel bearing are a sealed unit, you have to replace the whole unit if it goes bad, which fortunately isn't often.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 03-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||BBBAD GT||03-10-2000||06:13 PM||||Ordered the loaded calipers and rotors, went with the Bendix brand, came out to under $200 for all four wheels from carparts.com.  <P>My question is about master cylinders and SS lines.  The Fiero store had the "big bore" MC for around $50, which isn't bad.  I'm wondering what the 'big bore' was originally on, (ie stock for which car).  Also, do anybody have the SS lines from them also?  I'm assuming they just bolt up to the claipers/hard lines with no problems (even the Grand Am calipers).  Thanks for the responses.<P>(won't be my last post about these things, its just now coming together )  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||GREENDANO||03-10-2000||06:58 PM||||I have the SS brake lines on my F40.  They fit perfectly and look great. I got them from The Fiero Store.  It was the best price and unbelievable shipping.  I ordered them Tuesday, and had them Thursday!<BR>  E-mail me privately about your hub machining needs.  I don't usually discuss things like that in an open forum.<BR>  Dan-O<BR>dakers1@twcny.rr.com||||reg||14
Z||000006||batboy||03-10-2000||09:04 PM||||The "big bore" master cylinder is likely to just be a S-10 unit. Most people use that anyway with the Grand Am brakes and then they swap the Fiero brake fluid tank onto the S-10 M/C, looks factory. Sounds like you got a good deal on the calipers and rotors. I'm jealous of the SS hoses, I should of done that too, still might when I replace my M/C.||||reg||1
Z||000007||sully5||03-11-2000||11:27 PM||||The Grand Am upgrade sounds like the way to go. I am going to look at the SS lines. I appriciate all the resonse I got when I started my project. I opted to go with the stock as I wanted to get mine on the road quick and no shops here seemed to have done the kind of work that was going to be needed. I found stock rear calipers at Autozone for 33 ea. exchanged. CarQuest had the rotors at 31 ea. Next time I am definitely going with the upgrade. Thanks again for all the help yall have given.||||reg||1
